           'Elysium is a limited enclave where citizenship gives privileged access to automated healing and a robot-served, protected environment. Its residents obtain benefits that ordinary earnings on Earth cannot reliably buy. This supports a partial post-work provision record for the admitted cohort, especially in care and everyday service. It does not turn the entire system into a labor-free economy: human workers on Earth manufacture the robots and remain exposed to injury and exclusion.\n\nThe guarantee estimate concerns the enclave’s dependable technological support, with a wider uncertainty range because the film does not specify a universal free-income or housing entitlement. Effective control remains with wealthy humans and their political institutions; robots enforce and administer their choices rather than independently owning the whole order. Owning a residence or using a Med-Bay is not equal control over the station. Administrators and business owners still perform useful activity, so elite leisure cannot supply a population work percentage. The final change in medical eligibility is a different scope and receives its own restricted transition record.',
